diff --git a/docs/crm-import-verified-identities.md b/docs/crm-import-verified-identities.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..6ca55ca5f7b42ef956b74534c2598880622d2098
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/crm-import-verified-identities.md
@@ -0,0 +1,88 @@
+# Verified identities from existing Kommo imports
+
+## Bounded implementation
+
+An already-configured Kommo contact/lead read can now retain server-only
+provenance. The request still uses the existing URL, request count, payload,
+refresh and retry behavior. The credential store exposes the selected provider
+and connected_at from the same row snapshot as the token. No token or setting is
+changed by this addition.
+
+Only a direct successful GET of `contacts` or `leads`, with no redirect history
+and an unambiguous bounded page of canonical numeric IDs, produces an in-memory
+observation. The observation includes the actual successful attempt's tenant,
+account domain, connection generation, resource, observed IDs and an opaque
+request evidence ID. Refresh or a 401 retry uses the credentials of the final
+successful attempt, not a queued job's assumed account. Plain dicts remain
+compatible; JSON fields named `observation` are never accepted as evidence.
+
+The page transaction locks the tenant and all relevant integration rows, checks
+that this observation still matches the selected connection, then checks the
+existing job/run/connection fences. It retains those locks through import,
+verified mapping insertion and checkpoint commit. A stale response cannot be
+attached to a new generation; a valid new-generation response cannot be saved
+under an old job. A concurrent preferred OAuth INSERT is fenced even when only
+a manual connection existed when the read started.
+
+A verified mapping is registered only in the branch that just INSERTed the new
+native contact or deal, after the existing import policy, required-field and
+legacy-link checks. The mapping, native row, legacy link and page checkpoint share
+one transaction. Uniqueness conflicts abort that transaction, rather than
+reassigning an existing internal or external identity. Existing mapping evidence
+is retained on replay. No provider operation, grant or global gate is activated.
+
+## Deliberate non-adoption
+
+These paths never create verified mappings for:
+
+- A pre-existing native row found by a legacy provider ID.
+- A contact selected by phone/email matching, even if the remote record exists.
+- An existing imported row that lacks an observation, including rows imported
+  before this change. Reading the same numeric ID again is not verification.
+- A previously verified row after reconnect or account/auth-mode generation
+  change. The old binding remains historical and cannot authorize the new one.
+- Company, pipeline or stage identities, nested contact/company relationships,
+  browser-supplied metadata or BEIN provider records.
+
+The legacy import's existing matching and field-update behavior is unchanged.
+Registering a freshly imported deal does not certify its existing pipeline,
+stage, contact or company mappings. This is not a bulk backfill or a migration
+that upgrades old `saas_crm_entity_links` rows.
+
+## Audit of remaining hooks
+
+`tenant_crm_sync_outbox._send_unfenced` receives a naked create result, stores the
+legacy link in a later transaction and does not currently retain exact source
+binding. No create/export hook was added here. It needs a separately verified
+create receipt and current local-card/connection revalidation; ambiguous creates
+must remain unknown, never repeated to obtain an ID.
+
+`TenantKommo.request_bound` requires an already verified mapping and is not a
+bootstrap path. A successful record GET alone cannot prove a historical native
+record belongs to that remote record. The `owner_verified` storage primitive
+exists, but no browser verification UI or permission flow is implemented.
+
+BEIN's client has base URL/branch configuration, but no generation-bound identity
+receipt contract or supported relation mapping schema. There is no BEIN import
+path here and no safe BEIN registration hook. It remains unsupported.
+
+For existing records, the product choice is an explicit owner assertion of the
+exact native UUID, provider account/generation and remote entity/ID, with a
+current-owner check, current connection fence and durable evidence. Any remote
+read would confirm that exact asserted target, never choose one by name/phone or
+coincident numeric ID. Decide that UI and evidence contract before implementing
+it. Do not reuse automatic import as owner verification.
+
+## Verification
+
+Synthetic transport tests cover pre-send refresh, 401 retry, actual final source,
+manual/OAuth distinction, redirects, malformed/duplicate IDs, JSON spoofing and
+unchanged request count/body. Isolated SQL tests cover rollback with the page
+checkpoint, current/old job generations, tenant/provider/entity/ID mismatch,
+legacy and phone/email non-adoption, uniqueness collision and immutable evidence.
+
+The separate localhost PostgreSQL fence suite covers concurrent reconnect,
+preferred integration INSERT and duplicate imports. It requires an explicit
+`BEINTASKBOT_TEST_POSTGRES_DSN` and rejects non-local hosts. PGlite/WASM tests do
+not prove multi-session lock behavior. No production/provider calls or real
+customer records are used by these tests.
diff --git a/docs/crm-relation-delivery-safety.md b/docs/crm-relation-delivery-safety.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..dd81c0ceca2c52292eeb72c3e7e9c247229106cf
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/crm-relation-delivery-safety.md
@@ -0,0 +1,91 @@
+# Dormant relationship delivery
+
+## What is implemented
+
+The relation store changes a single native FK and inserts its durable intent and
+latest-head marker in the same transaction. It records the authenticated source
+card and actor, not merely the changed FK owner. Delivery rechecks the source
+card's update permission, active tenant/member, ownership/department scope,
+active records, exact local revision/head, current sync policy, verified mapping,
+account domain and connection generation. Tenant and row locks stay held through
+conditional provider I/O. The tenant key lock also fences insertion of a newly
+preferred integration while a manual connection is selected.
+
+Identity rows are separate from legacy unbound IDs. Import/create evidence or an
+explicit owner verification is required; reconnect never adopts old mappings
+just because the same numeric provider ID exists. Token refresh preserves the
+integration generation; a connection or account change invalidates it.
+
+Kommo planning uses only POST /leads/{id}/link and /unlink (or the corresponding
+contact/company endpoints), one target at a time. Replacing a main contact links
+the new main before detaching only the previous main. Secondary contacts remain
+in every planned state. Company replacement is explicitly two steps, not atomic.
+All remote snapshots include the complete relevant ID set and main flags; partial,
+paged, malformed or ambiguous snapshots fail closed. No record PATCH is used.
+
+The driver commits a sending marker before I/O and keeps a session advisory lock
+across commits. A duplicate worker cannot dispatch concurrently. Crash, timeout,
+unknown result, or lost read-back causes no replay and no compensating unlink.
+Even observing the desired state does not prove an earlier in-flight request has
+finished. A later invocation cannot infer permission from a queued event.
+
+## Deliberate activation blocker
+
+There is no production adapter or scheduled caller. Existing scoped relation
+requests continue to return 409 when outbound synchronization is configured.
+The journal API is dormant; the migration only creates additive empty tables.
+No identities are automatically backfilled and no live sync policy is changed.
+
+A valid delivery adapter must compare the full expected remote relation graph and
+apply the one narrow operation under an actual atomic/exclusive provider fence.
+The test mock does this in memory. Kommo's published link/unlink API does not
+supply a documented CAS/version precondition. Read/check/POST/read can detect
+some concurrent changes but cannot prevent overwriting a main-contact edit that
+arrives between the check and POST. Local PostgreSQL locks do not fence Kommo UI
+users or other integrations. This is a missing integration contract, not a new
+permanent restriction on the product or the user's approved relation semantics.
+
+The smallest missing safety contract is provider-supported conditional relation
+writes (or a verified exclusive-write mechanism covering *all* remote writers),
+plus a way to establish that an uncertain prior request has finished before any
+subsequent step. Without those guarantees, activating the current mock contract
+as ordinary Kommo HTTP would be unsafe. Do not replace it with a boolean flag or
+an in-process lock. Unlinking the only main while secondary contacts remain also
+needs verified provider behavior regarding automatic main-contact reassignment.
+
+BEIN has no independently verified narrow link/unlink contract here. It remains
+unsupported; do not invent PUT/PATCH semantics or route relation intents through
+the whole-record exporter. Contact-company activation additionally needs an
+explicit supported sync-policy field, since the current policy UI does not offer
+company_id for contact synchronization.
+
+## Scoped scalar provenance follow-up
+
+New scoped scalar mutations now have a separate atomic journal with actor,
+revision and immutable changed-field masks. See `crm-scoped-scalar-sync.md` for
+dispatch fences, narrow API receipts and the remaining activation blockers.
+Existing legacy event contents are not rewritten. Unknown historical provenance,
+in-flight legacy delivery, recovery UI and provider concurrency guarantees still
+need a rollout resolution. This is not a claim that the scoped gate can be enabled.
+
+## Verified primary references (2026-11-11)
+
+- https://developers.kommo.com/reference/linking-entities : POST relation endpoint;
+  response metadata main_contact.
+- https://developers.kommo.com/reference/unlinking-entities : POST unlink endpoint.
+- https://developers.kommo.com/reference/linked-entities : full links observation.
+- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/amocrm/amocrm-api-php/master/src/AmoCRM/Models/ContactModel.php :
+  getMetadataForLink serializes the request field is_main (not main_contact).
+
+These references establish endpoint shapes, not a concurrency or idempotency
+contract. No live provider requests were made during development.
+
+## Verification
+
+Pure contract/planner tests and isolated PostgreSQL/WASM tests exercise rollback,
+source-only update authorization, stale/revoked access, reconnect/account changes,
+mapping changes, local edits, secondary preservation, duplicate delivery,
+timeout-after-apply and simulated process loss. A separate localhost PostgreSQL
+suite exercises actual multi-session blocking and concurrent dispatch. It is
+skipped without BEINTASKBOT_TEST_POSTGRES_DSN; WASM is not evidence of real
+multi-session locking. Tests never use production databases or credentials.
diff --git a/docs/crm-scoped-scalar-sync.md b/docs/crm-scoped-scalar-sync.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..874beba6b1a19eaa3b939b95867cf388979bf2c5
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/crm-scoped-scalar-sync.md
@@ -0,0 +1,137 @@
+# Scoped scalar synchronization
+
+New human contact/deal mutations with an effective scoped profile now write a
+separate scalar journal in the same transaction as the native row mutation. The
+API does not enqueue a second actorless legacy event. A public `sync` receipt
+separates `local_saved` from external `pending`, `needs_verification`, `off` and
+`unchanged` states. No response claims external synchronization completed merely
+because the local card was saved.
+
+An event contains source actor/action, tenant/card, exact local revision, immutable
+changed-and-allowed scalar field mask, before/after values, content fingerprint,
+and verified account/generation/record mapping. Relations never enter its mask.
+Policy additions cannot expand an accepted event. Dispatch locks current source U,
+tenant/member/department, native record, integration, mappings and provider policy
+until the single PATCH/read-back completes. Later local revisions are rejected;
+no worker exports another actor's newer row values under an older event.
+
+The Kommo transport uses a single object PATCH for a mapped existing lead/contact,
+with only name or supported custom scalar fields. It does not call the legacy
+record exporter, POST/create, link/unlink, refresh tokens under locks, or replay an
+uncertain request. Known divergent remote field values conflict. Existing single
+phone/email category metadata is retained; multiple provider values are not
+flattened. Timeout, crash or unavailable read-back is unknown, never an automatic
+retry. A prior unknown/sending event blocks later writes for the same card.
+
+## Legacy behavior
+
+Records that have never had scoped provenance retain their original legacy queue
+and payload behavior, including gate-off operation. There is no blanket legacy
+queue pause and no global field stripping. A legacy event for a record with a scoped provenance head is rejected as
+`legacy_scoped_revision_conflict`, whether its revision is older, equal or newer.
+A later service/legacy phone edit cannot authorize exporting a prior scoped name
+through a full-record payload. This is record-specific quarantine, not successful
+sync or inferred service/human authority. Unrelated legacy/service cards are
+untouched. The per-record barrier persists when the rollout flag is disabled:
+rollback cannot erase a scoped value's provenance or authorize its export through
+an actorless event. No markers are deleted or reset. Writer provenance for
+subsequent mixed-writer events and an explicit resolution path for quarantined events remain activation blockers.
+Already in-flight actorless requests cannot be recalled. Historical provenance
+and a drain/transition plan remain prerequisites for activating scoped mode.
+
+## Explicit current limits, not successful synchronization
+
+- Legacy external IDs are never silently adopted as account-bound mappings.
+  Missing verified record mapping produces `needs_verification` after local save.
+- New scoped creates do not silently create a provider record under update
+  authority. They report `needs_verification` with their original create action.
+- BEIN account-bound scalar identity, pipeline/stage catalog bindings, and an
+  independently verified phone/email clearing contract are not implemented by
+  this change. These specific events report `needs_verification`; mapped Kommo
+  name/title/nonempty single phone/email edits can use the guarded scalar path.
+- Expiring OAuth requires a refresh outside the dispatch fence. The bound
+  transport neither refreshes nor retries a 401 while authorization locks are held.
+- A newer edit explicitly supersedes an older unsent event. Its original values
+  and unsent outcome remain in the journal. The new receipt includes
+  `review_required` and `superseded_unsent_event_ids`. The new event contains only
+  its own changed fields and never silently coalesces another actor's unsent values.
+- Contact/deal save paths now display the receipt in RU/AZ/EN, distinguish local
+  save from queued/unverified/conflicting external synchronization, and explicitly
+  warn about an earlier unsent value. A separate notice survives list refresh and
+  remains visible inside a partially completed form. It makes no provider reads,
+  retries or mapping changes. Auth revalidation clears old-context receipts.
+- Native contact/deal/company cards now have a persisted, read-only event history,
+  authorized by current R of the exact source card. It shows the latest stored
+  outcome for each event, with immutable event identity/local revision and safe
+  issue labels. It does not display payloads, actors or provider/account metadata.
+  A recorded completion is not a fresh check of the external CRM. Empty history
+  does not establish successful sync or mean that no older external work exists.
+- There is still no recovery or mapping-verification UI. History cannot retry,
+  resolve unknown outcomes, clear barriers or adopt legacy mappings. Those
+  contracts and the existing provider/rollout prerequisites remain blockers.
+- Kommo's scalar PATCH, like its existing legacy scalar writes, does not provide
+  documented remote CAS. Pre-read conflict detection cannot eliminate a remote
+  edit between GET and PATCH. Narrow scalar payloads cannot alter relations, but
+  a stronger guarantee against same-field remote races needs a provider contract.
+
+No production policies or rights are changed, the global gate remains off, and
+no production credentials or provider requests were used during development.
+
+Primary API shapes checked:
+https://developers.kommo.com/reference/update-contact
+https://developers.kommo.com/reference/updating-single-lead
+https://developers.kommo.com/reference/custom-fields
+
+Tests include isolated PostgreSQL/WASM, mocked transport/API routing, rollback,
+revocation, changed provider policy, connection generation, stale revisions,
+unknown-outcome ordering and successive different-field edits. Real multi-session
+PostgreSQL fence tests require a disposable localhost DSN; they are skipped when
+unavailable, not represented as passing concurrency evidence.
+
+
+## Legacy-to-scoped admission fence
+
+Legacy delivery now owns a per-card PostgreSQL session advisory lock across the
+provider call and its local result commit. Native human contact/deal updates,
+when the scoped gate is enabled, take the matching transaction lock before any
+tenant/member/card locks. Thus the first scoped mutation waits for a currently
+running fenced legacy request; different cards can still proceed. Busy duplicate
+workers do not send or finish another worker's claim. After admission, the worker
+also validates the persisted processing state and attempt counter, not just an
+expired lease.
+
+New scoped-era claims atomically record `not_started` with the exact claim attempt
+before worker admission. A busy card therefore leaves durable evidence that this
+claim never sent. A later claim may renew only matching unsent evidence; it cannot
+replace another sender's `sending`/`unknown` outcome or invent evidence for a
+historical attempt. Send admission locks the outbox row while validating the
+claim and committing possible-send evidence. An expired unsent Kommo create is
+also safe to admit for its first actual send, unlike a possible earlier create.
+
+In scoped mode, legacy possible-send evidence is committed before I/O. Exceptions,
+process death and lost replies leave unknown/sending evidence, including when the
+old retry scheduler later puts the event back into pending. Such an event cannot
+be replayed, even after disabling the flag. A historical retry with no durable
+send evidence is also unverified rather than replayed while scoped mode is on.
+Unmarked gate-off records retain their existing successful exporter payload and
+retry behavior, with same-card serialization added.
+
+A first scoped local save with a processing/unknown legacy event, an attempted
+pending legacy retry, or unresolved possible-send evidence reports
+`needs_verification` / `scalar_legacy_outcome_unknown`. The scalar dispatcher
+rechecks this before any provider call. Processing/pending claims with matching
+durable `not_started` evidence are excluded, so ordinary card contention cannot
+permanently quarantine an unsent event or block a new scoped scalar edit. Unknown
+states and possible-send evidence are never excluded. Neither lease expiry nor a new local
+revision proves that a remote operation has stopped. There is no automatic
+recovery or clearing of this evidence.
+
+This is a bounded fence for the native human update entry points and this legacy
+delivery worker. Native stores open their own connections and do not accept an
+existing transaction. Actorless service writes, imports and direct SQL writers
+have not been globally converted or granted human authority. No lock is added to
+their tenant/config-first paths. New/updated workers must be fully rolled out and
+old in-flight workers drained before activation: an old worker cannot honor a
+new advisory lock. Historical ambiguous operations still require an explicit
+resolution contract. The fence is not remote CAS and does not prevent provider
+users or other applications from editing concurrently.
